WEBVTT

00:00:00.151 --> 00:00:03.510
Defenders should think in terms of data flows, not only servers.

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:07.870
Sensitive data may move over internet traffic, VPN tunnels, API calls, email,

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:12.458
file transfers, cloud storage synchronization, database replication, partner integrations,

00:00:12.458 --> 00:00:15.690
service-to-service communication, mobile application traffic,

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:17.060
and IoT or embedded device links.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:21.265
A server may be well managed, but the data path can still include networks, proxies, logs,

00:00:21.265 --> 00:00:25.000
archives, third-party services, and client environments that shape exposure.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.950
TLS-protected web traffic is a common place to start because websites, APIs, gateways,

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:33.000
and service meshes often carry valuable information.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.460
VPN traffic is also relevant because it may include administrative access,

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:39.000
internal applications, or remote workforce activity.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.055
Email and file transfer systems can move legal records, contracts, health data,

00:00:43.055 --> 00:00:45.000
credentials, and business plans.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.885
Backup and replication traffic can carry large volumes of sensitive information over

00:00:48.885 --> 00:00:50.000
predictable paths.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.789
Logs and archives can matter as much as live traffic.

00:00:54.789 --> 00:00:56.980
A network appliance, proxy, packet capture platform, troubleshooting workflow,

00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:00.985
security tool, or cloud service may preserve metadata or payloads longer than expected.

00:01:00.985 --> 00:01:04.000
Third-party systems can also change the exposure model.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.980
If sensitive information crosses a partner integration, managed service, CDN,

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.000
cloud storage platform, or SaaS product,

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.850
the organization's risk depends partly on external cryptographic and retention practices.

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:19.090
A practical assessment maps where sensitive data is created, transmitted, transformed,

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:21.000
stored, replicated, and retained.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.795
That map should include encryption boundaries, termination points, certificate use,

00:01:25.795 --> 00:01:30.000
VPN endpoints, API gateways, service identities, backup flows, and vendor-managed paths.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.000
The goal is not to assume every path is equally urgent.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.230
The goal is to find the paths where long-lived sensitive data could be collected today and

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:36.920
exposed later.
